    intro: "A jar of proper brined cucumbers or cherry jam is nearly impossible to buy in a shop: shelves hold vinegar marinades and jams with thickeners, while at home everything is made in brine, with dill from the garden, following grandmother's notebook. That jar used to come from a neighbor or from the dacha. Celina brings that exchange back to the city: homemade preserves are made and sold by people who live near you. Below: what people order in season, how to choose a jar and store it properly, how homemade differs from store-bought — and what to do if you're the one with too much harvest this year.",
